1
00:03:19,354 --> 00:03:25,354
He's fighting for everyone who can't fight back.

2
00:06:38,709 --> 00:06:44,709
Braddock: Missing in Action III is a action, war film directed by Aaron Norris.

3
00:09:58,064 --> 00:10:04,064
When Colonel James Braddock is told that his Asian wife and 12-year-old son are still
alive in Communist Vietnam, he mounts a one-man assault to free them.

4
00:13:17,419 --> 00:13:23,419
Armed with the latest high-tech firepower, Braddock fights his way into the heart of the country and
ends up battling his way out with several dozen abused Amerasian children in tow!

5
00:16:36,774 --> 00:16:42,774
Struggling to keep them alive while outmaneuvering a sadistic Vietnamese officer,
Braddock ignites the jungle in a blazing cross-country race for freedom.

6
00:19:56,129 --> 00:20:02,129
Col. James Braddock, played by Chuck Norris.

7
00:23:15,483 --> 00:23:21,483
Gen. Quoc, played by Aki Aleong.

8
00:26:34,838 --> 00:26:40,838
Rev. Polanski, played by Yehuda Efroni.

9
00:29:54,193 --> 00:30:00,193
Gen. Duncan, played by Floyd Levine.

10
00:33:13,548 --> 00:33:19,548
Littlejohn, played by Jack Rader.

11
00:36:32,903 --> 00:36:38,903
Embassy Gate Captain, played by Keith David.

12
00:39:52,258 --> 00:39:58,258
Braddock: Missing in Action III  is a 1988 American action adventure film, and the
third and final installment in the Missing in Action film series.

13
00:43:11,612 --> 00:43:17,612
The film stars Chuck Norris, who co-wrote the screenplay with James Bruner.

14
00:46:30,967 --> 00:46:36,967
The film was directed by Norris' brother, Aaron Norris.

15
00:49:50,322 --> 00:49:56,322
The film is about Colonel James Braddock learns from a missionary that his wife
Lin, long presumed dead, is alive and that he has a 12-year-old son.

16
00:53:09,677 --> 00:53:15,677
Suspicious of a CIA official’s attempt to dismiss the claim,
Braddock returns to Vietnam to investigate.

17
00:56:29,032 --> 00:56:35,032
Cannon Films, facing financial trouble, pushed for another Missing
in Action movie, but Chuck Norris initially refused.

18
00:59:48,387 --> 00:59:54,387
His interest was revived when his brother Aaron told him about the thousands of Amerasian children left behind in
Vietnam, inspiring Norris to research the issue and get emotionally invested in telling their story.

19
01:03:07,741 --> 01:03:13,741
The project originally began under director Joseph Zito,
but creative conflicts led to his departure.

20
01:06:27,096 --> 01:06:33,096
Jack Smight was briefly attached before Aaron Norris
ultimately made his directorial debut with the film.

21
01:09:46,451 --> 01:09:52,451
Production took place in the Philippines, where a tragic helicopter crash
claimed the lives of four military and police officers.

22
01:13:05,806 --> 01:13:11,806
Despite the setbacks, Norris believed before its release that Braddock:
Missing in Action III was the best film he had made.

23
01:16:25,161 --> 01:16:31,161
Braddock: Missing in Action III opened at number five at the U.S.

24
01:19:44,516 --> 01:19:50,516
box office, earning $2.2 million in its first weekend.

25
01:23:03,870 --> 01:23:09,870
Despite Chuck Norris’s belief that it was the strongest entry in the
series, it became the least successful financially.

26
01:26:23,225 --> 01:26:29,225
Norris later expressed frustration with Cannon Films’ marketing efforts,
claiming they mishandled the release and jeopardized his career.

27
01:29:42,580 --> 01:29:48,580
The film was met with mostly negative reviews, criticized for its formulaic
action, excessive violence, and lack of emotional depth.

28
01:33:01,935 --> 01:33:07,935
Many viewed it as a routine entry in Chuck Norris’s filmography,
offering little beyond familiar one-man-army tropes.

29
01:36:21,290 --> 01:36:27,290
Some noted faint attempts at emotional storytelling, but overall, it was seen as
predictable and heavy-handed rather than impactful or engaging.

30
01:39:40,645 --> 01:39:46,645
The film explores themes of vietnam war, vietnam
veteran, martial arts, orphanage, patriotism.
